# Activity Card

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

The Activity card lists what has happened to the things you choose, newest first, such as “Door unlocked” or “Light turned on”. Use it so people can check what happened in a room or to a device without leaving the dashboard.

This page owns the card that Home Assistant calls Activity. For the card surface see [Card (Base Surface)](cards.html).

This page covers:

- Activity Card
  - `hui-logbook-card`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

From top to bottom:

1. An optional title, with a “Show more” arrow link to the full Activity page for the same period.
2. A warning, if a chosen device cannot be found.
3. A scrolling list of entries, each with a name, a description and a time. The card draws no icon at the start of an entry.

If the Logbook integration is not loaded, the card shows a warning instead. With nothing to show it says “No activity found.”

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-hui-logbook-card"><code>hui-logbook-card</code></h3>

The card that Home Assistant shows as “Activity” is `hui-logbook-card`. Its YAML type keeps the older name, `logbook`.

```yaml
type: logbook
title: Front door activity
target:
  entity_id:
    - lock.front_door
    - binary_sensor.front_door
hours_to_show: 24
```

Keys:

- `target` is required. It can hold entities, devices, areas, floors or labels. An empty target gives an error. The older `entities` key still works.
- `title` is optional.
- `hours_to_show` is the period, 24 by default.
- `state_filter` limits entries to chosen states.
- `name_detail` controls how much of the name is repeated in each entry. The default leaves the name out when one entity is chosen and adds the device or area when several are.
- `theme` applies a theme to this card.

The card is 385 pixels tall unless the layout sets the height, and the list scrolls inside it.

**What the Civica theme sets.** The card surface, the title (through the `ha-card-header-*` keys) and the text and divider colours. See [Card (Base Surface)](cards.html).

**What stays as Home Assistant draws it.** The list entries, their times and the scrolling list have no theme variable. Changing them would need the card-mod integration. Test any selector in your own dashboard.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- It is the native card and reads the same history as the Activity page.
- The “Show more” link opens the full list.
- The title is drawn as a level-one heading, so heading order on the page may need care.
- The list scrolls in a fixed-height box. Check that keyboard users can reach every entry on your own dashboard.
- The card does not say how recent the list is unless you do. Do not promise live updates.
- Entries have no icon at the start, so each one must say in words what happened.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Choose a small target, so the list answers one question, such as who used the front door. Put the period in the title or beside it. Avoid repeating the same name on every line by leaving `name_detail` on its default. Tell people when there is no activity, for example “Nothing has happened in the last 24 hours.” See [Dashboard composition and card features](../home-assistant/dashboard-composition.html) for where to place it.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the title is a heading. Home Assistant does not draw the entries as a list, so give each one a clear name and time.
- 1.3.3 Sensory Characteristics and 1.4.1 Use of Colour: do not rely on the colour of the timeline marker.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: the fixed height scrolls; test at large text.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard and 2.4.3 Focus Order: check that the scrolling list can be reached.
- 2.4.4 Link Purpose: the “Show more” link has a text name.
- 3.2.4 Consistent Identification: use the same title style for every Activity card.

